  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the retrieval:\n\n**Error origin \u2014 `pkg/registry/core/rest/storage_core.go`**: the `\"unable to perform initial IP and Port allocation check\"` error (and the PostStartHook name `\"start-service-ip-repair-controllers\"`) live in `legacyProvider.PostStartHook()`. It launches the ClusterIP and NodePort repair controllers via `async.Runner`, waits on a `sync.WaitGroup` for both `onFirstSuccess` callbacks, and returns the error only if that doesn't happen within 1 minute.\n\n**Propagation chain**:\n1. `pkg/registry/core/service/ipallocator/controller/repairip.go` (`RepairIPAddress.RunUntil`) \u2014 waits for its own informers (Service, ServiceCIDR, IPAddress) to sync, polls for the default `ServiceCIDR` object to exist, then runs `doRunOnce()`/`syncService()`, which calls `client.NetworkingV1beta1().IPAddresses().Create(...)` to repair any missing `IPAddress` object for an existing Service's ClusterIP \u2014 this is where a create for an IP like `10.211.95.185` originates. Only after this succeeds does it call `onFirstSuccess()`.\n2. That create request goes back through the same (or peer) apiserver's admission chain. `staging/.../admission/plugin/webhook/generic/webhook.go` and `staging/.../admission/plugin/policy/generic/plugin.go` both gate `Dispatch()` on `WaitForReady()` (synced namespace informer + hook/policy source informer); if not ready they return `admission.NewForbidden(a, fmt.Errorf(\"not yet ready to handle request\"))` \u2014 this is the exact string in the ticket. (`namespace/lifecycle/admission.go` has the same string but short-circuits for non-namespaced resources like `IPAddress`, so it isn't the source here.)\n3. If the repair controller's create keeps failing/retrying past 1 minute, `wg.Done()`/`onFirstSuccess` never fires, the `select` in the PostStartHook times out, and it returns the `\"unable to perform initial IP and Port allocation check\"` error.\n4. `staging/.../server/hooks.go` (`runPostStartHook`) treats any non-nil PostStartHook error as fatal (`klog.Fatalf`) and, prior to that, leaves `entry.done` unclosed. `postStartHookHealthz.Check()` returns `errHookNotFinished` while `done` is open, which surfaces as the `poststarthook/start-service-ip-repair-controllers` readyz check failing.\n5. `staging/.../server/healthz/healthz.go`'s `informerSync` checker independently produces the `informer-sync` readyz failure, and `handleRootHealth` reports both as `[-]informer-sync ... [-]poststarthook/start-service-ip-repair-controllers ... check failed: readyz`, matching the ticket.\n\n**The race during a mixed-version rolling upgrade**: a newly-upgraded v1.33 apiserver starts its own admission plugins (webhook/policy generic plugins) and its repair controller concurrently. The repair controller's `IPAddresses().Create()` call hits the *same* apiserver's admission stack before that apiserver's own informers/webhook sources have finished their initial sync (`WaitForReady()` false) \u2014 a self-inflicted \"not ready to accept the request that its own readiness gate depends on\" cycle. Because `NewRepairIPAddress`'s repair loop also blocks on the default `ServiceCIDR` object existing (`wait.PollUntilContextCancel` against `serviceCIDRLister`) before it can even attempt the create, any delay in that CIDR informer syncing during the mixed-version window compounds the same 1-minute race. Once all nodes are upgraded and informers/caches are consistently warm cluster-wide, the create succeeds quickly, `onFirstSuccess` fires well under a minute, and the hook \u2014 and readyz \u2014 go green.",
